WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.960
When you picture a "grizzled police&nbsp;
officer," few TV cops fit the bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.160
as well as Jason Beghe's "Chicago P.D."&nbsp;
character Squad Commander Hank Voight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.360
But that grizzled voice isn't an acting choice&nbsp;
– it's a reminder of a near-death experience.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.680
Hank Voight is a classic example of the well&nbsp;
worn "cynical loose cannon" archetype. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.000
Beghe knows how to look and act tough. But best&nbsp;
of all, he has that unforgettably rough-sounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.920
voice to convincingly sell his crime-fighting&nbsp;
persona on screen. Only a chain-smoking gargoyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.400
who drinks broken glass for breakfast could&nbsp;
hope to match the gravitas in Beghe's voice.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.280
"We don't have enough to get a&nbsp;
search warrant for his damn house!"

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.920
Beghe's role as Hank Voight began in&nbsp;
Season 1 of "Chicago Fire." Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.600
the character has become iconic, making&nbsp;
guest appearances in several other network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.560
television procedurals, including in "Law&nbsp;
&amp; Order: Special Victims Unit," "Chicago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.720
Med," and "Chicago Justice" as well as&nbsp;
"Chicago P.D." When fans first meet his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.080
character in "Chicago Fire," Voight&nbsp;
seems to be a dirty cop who covers up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.240
criminal activities for self-interest. But as&nbsp;
"Chicago P.D." and future appearances confirm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.440
there is much more bubbling beneath the surface&nbsp;
of this complicated law enforcement leader.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.160
"You're telling me you won't give me a warrant&nbsp;
based on a tip from a reliable informant."

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.600
And as fans know, Beghe's voice is&nbsp;
pitch-perfect for Voight's character. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.680
it's not a voice that he's had to tweak over&nbsp;
time to perfect. That's his real-life voice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.600
What "Chicago P.D." fans might not know is&nbsp;
that his voice wasn't always so pleasantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.040
rough on the ears. His distinctively gritty&nbsp;
sound is the result of a car accident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.600
the star had in 1999 – and that near-death&nbsp;
experience forever altered the actor's life.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.240
In an interview with The Daily Beast,&nbsp;
Beghe explained that his naturally deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.560
voice became exacerbated as a result&nbsp;
of the terrible car accident. He said:

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.320
"[I] broke my neck, my back in a couple of&nbsp;
places, all of my ribs, both of my lungs. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.920
was in a coma for three and a half weeks. When I&nbsp;
would come out of the coma — since I was intubated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.400
from being on life support — I would pull the&nbsp;
tube out and it made it even more f---ed up."

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.760
The change in tone is clear when you&nbsp;
look at some of his pre-accident roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.780
like his guest turn on Melrose Place.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.720
"It kind of flies in the face&nbsp;
of that 'all men are created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.680
equal' thing I dedicated my life to defend."

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.120
The fact that this brush with death would&nbsp;
help Beghe play his most famous role years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:12.360
down the line is a silver lining to&nbsp;
an otherwise dire situation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.200
his deep voice comes in handy for more&nbsp;
than just playing "bad cop." Within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.160
same interview, Beghe joked that his&nbsp;
voice also helps him play tough Dad.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.760
"When I say, 'Hey, it's time&nbsp;
to clean up,' they do it."

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.120
Beyond the accident giving him the&nbsp;
perfect voice for a morally gray cop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.440
Beghe acknowledges how the accident had a serious&nbsp;
effect on his life. In an interview with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.840
Pittsburgh Post-Gazette in 2021, Beghe discussed&nbsp;
how the car accident affected him psychologically.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.360
"Technically, I did die for a short period&nbsp;
of time when I was in the coma. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.320
definitely an experience that profoundly informed&nbsp;
my perception of what life is. Twenty years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.960
it's not something I think about very&nbsp;
often. But as you bring it up to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.240
I do believe that I've probably&nbsp;
permanently changed since then."

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.760
Perhaps that is why Beghe appreciates&nbsp;
Voight's direct demeanor, which pushes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.240
his fellow "Chicago P.D." characters to&nbsp;
live authentically and stand by their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:04.440
point of view. In 2023, Beghe reflected on&nbsp;
Voight's no-nonsense character, stating,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.880
"One thing that Voight insists on and&nbsp;
admires — and it's hard for his team to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.960
do — is he always wants you to be&nbsp;
you. Don't be me. He doesn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.320
a team of Voights [...] It can create&nbsp;
some tension but it does create trust."

00:03:16.447 --> 00:03:20.440
"The right thing? Are you kidding me?&nbsp;
You want to talk about the right thing?!"

